<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conspiracy of Cinadon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/399-bce">399 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Cinadon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The conspiracy of Cinadon was an attempted coup d'état in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 399 BCE, early in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a>. Its leader, Cinadon, was a member of the king's bodyguard but not one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">homoioi</a>, the full citizens, and he drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neodamodes">neodamodes</a>, hypomeiones and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioeci</a> into a plot to break the power of the Spartan elite. The informer who betrayed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> said that Cinadon had counted only 40 full citizens among some 4,000 people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>. The ephors sent Cinadon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Aulon</a> in Messenia on a pretext, had him questioned there and arrested his accomplices; he was whipped through the city and possibly executed. The main source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s Hellenica.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name is known from Xenophon’s account of the conspiracy of 399 BCE led by <b>Cinadon</b>, a young man “not one of the equals”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
